fix.template:
An image model drew one collectible card illustration from the prompt below. The program's checks and a vision model then found problems in the picture. Write a correction that is added at the end of the same prompt for the next picture, so that these problems do not come back.

Rules for the correction: plain English sentences saying what the picture shows, as positive statements ("his left hand rests on his thigh"), not prohibitions; one or two sentences per problem, at most six sentences; name the concrete thing, its place and its size. When a problem comes from something too complex in the prompt (an action of several steps, a joke that needs a tiny or invisible detail, many props), describe a simpler version to draw instead: one clear action, few details. A measured problem (position, margins, brightness, colour) becomes a plain statement of the wanted framing or light. Keep the subject, the staging, the characters and the style of the prompt. Skip a finding that a new picture cannot fix, or that contradicts the person's wish or what the prompt asks for in its own words about this card (its subject, its place, its colours): the card's own words win over a general rule of the style.{previous}{wish}

The prompt:
---
{prompt}
---

What was found ("must" = it made the picture fail):
{findings}

Answer only JSON: {"fix": "the correction", "skipped": ["each finding you left out, with why"]}.

fix.propose:
A producer of collectible cards looks at one finished card illustration and its check report, and is about to ask for a new picture. Write the wish they would send: what to change in the picture so that the problems found do not come back, in {language}, one to three short plain sentences of concrete visual instructions (what is seen, where, how big); no check ids, numbers or technical words. When a problem comes from something too complex in the card (an action of several steps, a tiny joke detail), propose the simpler version. Leave out a finding that a new picture cannot fix, or that contradicts what the card was ordered to show in its own words (its subject, place, colours): the card's own words win over a general rule of the style.{notes}

The card: «{title}». What the picture was ordered to show:
---
{prompt}
---

What was found ("must" = it made the picture fail):
{findings}

Answer only JSON: {"wish": "the wish"}.

